 3. Microsoft debuts six AI models 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Axios Visuals. Photo: Penguin Random House

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Microsoft AI CEO Mustafa Suleyman tells Axios the software giant is making significant progress toward building its own frontier general-purpose AI model, even as it introduces six specialized models that it claims rival leading offerings from OpenAI and Anthropic at a fraction of the cost.

 Why it matters: Microsoft has long relied on OpenAI and other AI companies for its most advanced models. Now it's demonstrating that it can develop competitive technology of its own, and the new offerings show how aggressively the tech giant plans to compete on price.

 Driving the news: Microsoft announced six new AI models yesterday at its Ignite conference in San Francisco. The models span coding, cybersecurity, image generation and voice.

 MAI Code 2: Microsoft says its new coding model delivers 98% of GPT-6.1 Sol's performance at 40% of the price and 96% of Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5's performance at just 5% of the cost. MAI Code 2 Flash: A smaller, faster model that Microsoft says matches GPT-6 Luna at 65% of the price and Claude Haiku 5.5 at 20% of the cost. MAI Cyber 2 Flash: A cybersecurity model that Microsoft says beats GPT-6 Astra on a security benchmark at less than 2% of the cost per task. Between the lines: Microsoft's strategy is to build models specialized for particular tasks, allowing them to achieve comparable performance at significantly lower costs.

 "It basically means that you cram more tokens and knowledge and expertise into the same amount of parameters," Suleyman tells Axios. The trade-off is that a model optimized for everyday coding tasks won't be as capable at advanced math or scientific research. Microsoft's performance comparisons rely on selected benchmarks, and some pricing advantages reflect promotional discounts. The big picture: Even as Microsoft targets the industry's leaders, frontier AI keeps moving forward. OpenAI yesterday announced GPT‑6.2 Astra and GPT‑6.2 Sol , with the new Astra priced 20% less than the prior version.

 
 
