 4. Why Dems want to slow Anthropic&#x27;s $1T IPO 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A group of elected officials from five states led by Democrats is seeking to pressure Anthropic to square its warnings of catastrophic risk with plans to go public.

 Why it matters: The questions for the company aren't likely to stop the IPO, but they are a preview of the scrutiny tech giants can expect to face after the midterms' blue AI earthquake .

 While Democrats have yet to build a comprehensive AI policy platform, it's already clear that many do not favor acceleration. Elected officials and fiscal stewards in Maryland, Illinois, Colorado, Connecticut and New Mexico are demanding that Anthropic tackle crucial issues before it starts trading, according to a letter sent to CEO Dario Amodei and the Anthropic board.

 "Innovation cannot come at the expense of accountability and transparency, especially when the retirements of our teachers, firefighters, police and all state workers are on the line," Maryland State Comptroller Brooke Lierman said in a statement. The solution the state officials propose is to bake in a sunset of the founders' rights and to disclose "far more" about how Anthropic's Long-Term Benefit Trust would operate given that its trustees elect directors.

